* Notrump openings

Booklet sections "GENERAL APPROACH", "RESPONSES AND LATER BIDDING AFTER A 15--17 1NT
OPENING", "INTERFERENCE AFTER A 1NT OPENING BID" and "RESPONSES TO A 2NT OR A 3NT
OPENING". A notrump opening may contain a 5-card suit, even a major. 2NT and 3NT
continue the same ladder (2NT = 20--21, 3NT = 25--27) and outrank 2!c for an
equivalent balanced hand, since SAYC treats the direct notrump bid as the more
specific call for those two ranges (see NOTES.md #2).

1N = {prio:90} 15--17 hcp, bal
2N = {prio:95} 20--21 hcp, bal
3N = {prio:101} 25--27 hcp, bal

** Responses to 1NT

Stayman is non-forcing (the auction may stop in 2 of a suit); opener bids 2H with
4-4 in the majors. Jacoby transfers are used for both majors; 2S is a relay to
3C for a weak hand with one long minor: opener must rebid 3C, and responder
passes with clubs or corrects to 3D with diamonds (see NOTES.md #13). Without a
major, responder raises by strength: 2NT invites (8--9), 3NT is to play
(10--15), 4NT is the quantitative slam invitation (16--17) and 6NT is to play
(18--19); 4C (Gerber, NOTES.md #15) is kept for the rare 20+ hand. Every leaf of
this tree lists opener's closing Pass explicitly (NOTES.md "Notrump lane" #N1),
so a sign-off such as 1NT-3NT is a system position, not a fall-through to
natural inference.

